{{!
    @template local_byblos/docs/publishing

    Guide: the draft -> published workflow.

    Context variables:
    - links (object) Map of topic key => URL.
    - dashurl (string) Portfolio dashboard URL.
    - newpageurl (string) New-page wizard URL.

    @package    local_byblos
    @copyright  2026 South African Theological Seminary
    @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later

    Example context (json):
    {
        "dashurl": "#",
        "newpageurl": "#",
        "pix": {"publish_draft": "#"},
        "links": {
            "index": "#", "getting_started": "#", "building_pages": "#",
            "publishing": "#", "collections": "#", "sharing": "#",
            "assessment": "#", "usecase_programme": "#", "usecase_course": "#",
            "usecase_professional": "#"
        }
    }
}}
<div class="byblos-docs-article">

    <h1>{{#str}}docs_topic_publishing, local_byblos{{/str}}</h1>
    <p class="byblos-docs-lead">
        Every page is either a <strong>draft</strong> or <strong>published</strong>. Drafts are private to
        you while you work; publishing makes a page visible to the people you have shared it with.
        Publishing and sharing are two separate steps, you need both.
    </p>

    <h2>Draft vs published</h2>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li><strong>Draft</strong>: only you can see it, no matter who you have shared it with. New pages always start as drafts.</li>
        <li><strong>Published</strong>: visible to anyone you have shared the page (or its collection) with, and to anyone holding a public link.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="byblos-docs-note">
        <strong>Sharing alone is not enough.</strong> If you share a draft, your viewers still see nothing
        until you publish. Likewise, publishing without <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">sharing</a> means
        nobody has been granted access yet. Do both.
    </div>

    <h2>Publishing a page</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Open the page (from the dashboard, click the page card, or use Preview from the editor).</li>
        <li>In the action bar, click <strong>{{#str}}publishpage, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
        <li>Confirm when prompted, <em>"{{#str}}confirmpublishpage, local_byblos{{/str}}"</em></li>
        <li>The status changes to published and the page becomes visible to your viewers.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="byblos-docs-figure">
        <img src="{{pix.publish_draft}}" class="byblos-docs-img" loading="lazy"
             alt="The page action bar showing the draft status badge and the green Publish button.">

        <figcaption>Figure 1: Publishing a draft page.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Reverting to draft</h2>
    <p>Need to take a page back offline, to fix something, or after an assessment window closes?</p>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Open the published page.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>{{#str}}reverttodraft, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
        <li>Confirm, <em>"{{#str}}confirmreverttodraft, local_byblos{{/str}}"</em></li>
        <li>The page is hidden from viewers again until you re-publish.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="byblos-docs-tip">
        <strong>Check before you publish.</strong> Use <strong>Preview</strong> from the editor or the page
        action bar to see your page exactly as a viewer will. Byblos also offers a
        "preview as viewer" mode so you can confirm what a shared person, not you, the owner, will actually see.
    </div>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>With the page published, decide who can see it in <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">Sharing</a>.</p>
</div>
